<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perseus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Perseús</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In Greek mythology, Perseus is the founder of the Perseid dynasty and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellerophon">Bellerophon</a>, the chief Greek hero and slayer of monsters before the days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. The son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and the mortal Danaë, he was cast into the sea with his mother by her father, King Acrisius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, whom an oracle had warned he would be killed by his grandson, and was raised on Seriphos by the fisherman Dictys. He beheaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgon">Gorgon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a> for King Polydectes and saved Andromeda from the sea monster Cetus. In one account he later killed Acrisius by accident with a quoit, fulfilling the oracle, and Pausanias asserts that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> believed Perseus founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perseus.jpg" alt="Perseus" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perseo_con_la_testa_di_medusa,_originale_di_età_flavia_o_traianea,_dalle_terme_presso_porta_laurentina,_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the myth, King Acrisius of Argos had only a daughter, Danaë, and consulted the Oracle at Delphi about an heir. He shut Danaë in a room in a bronze tower, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> came to her as a shower of gold and fathered Perseus, who is therefore also called Chrysopatros, gold-fathered. Unwilling to kill the child of Zeus and provoke the gods and the Erinyes, Acrisius cast mother and son into the sea in a wooden chest.</p><p>They washed ashore on the island of Seriphos, where the fisherman Dictys raised the boy. Dictys's brother Polydectes, the king, came to desire Danaë, and Perseus protected her. Polydectes then held a banquet for which each guest owed a gift and, when Perseus named the head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a>, held him to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> equipped Perseus: winged sandals, the harpe sword and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>'s helm of darkness from Hermes, and a polished shield and a knapsack from Athena. He took the one eye of the Graeae, three old women who shared it, and returned it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgon">Gorgons</a>' location. Looking only at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a>'s reflection in Athena's shield, he cut off her head. From her neck sprang her children by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>, the winged horse Pegasus and the giant Chrysaor, and he escaped her sisters Stheno and Euryale by wearing the helm.</p><p>In Aethiopia he found Andromeda chained to a rock as a sacrifice to the sea monster Cetus, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> after her mother Cassiopeia boasted of her beauty. He killed the monster with the harpe, in other versions with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a>'s head, and married her despite her earlier engagement to Phineus, whom the head turned to stone. Back on Seriphos he petrified Polydectes and made Dictys king, then gave the head to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, who set it on her aegis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Accounts of the fulfilment of the oracle differ. In Pausanias, Perseus was showing off his newly invented quoit at games at Larissa when Acrisius, a visitor, stepped into its path. In the Bibliotheca, Acrisius went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, and Perseus's discus throw at funeral games struck him. Ashamed to rule Argos after causing a death, Perseus exchanged kingdoms with Megapenthes and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiryns">Tiryns</a>.</p><p>Pausanias says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> believed Perseus founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> as his capital, and he describes a shrine of Perseus on the road to Argos and the spring Persea. Perseus and Andromeda had seven sons and two daughters, and their line, the Perseids, ruled Mycenae and included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. According to the Suda, Perseus later founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> and died after turning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a>'s head on himself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Perseus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
